---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro";
import Chapter from "../components/Chapter.astro";
import Experience from "../components/Experience.astro";
import Services from "../components/Services.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";
import RahatDemo from "../components/demos/RahatDemo.astro";
import PaymentsDemo from "../components/demos/PaymentsDemo.astro";
import LabelingDemo from "../components/demos/LabelingDemo.astro";
import SrDemo from "../components/demos/SrDemo.astro";
import SwiperDemo from "../components/demos/SwiperDemo.astro";
---
<Base>
  <nav class="nav">
    <div class="wrap row">
      <a class="logo" href="#top" aria-label="Back to top"><span class="tick">✓</span> licman</a>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#work">Work</a></li>
        <li><a href="#experience">Experience</a></li>
        <li><a href="#hire">Hire me</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </nav>
  <main id="top">
    <Hero />

    <div id="work">
      <Chapter
        id="rahat"
        num="01"
        kicker="Product · 2026"
        title="Rahat Go: a bakery that runs inside Telegram"
        accent="#e8962a"
        stats={[
          { value: "3+ months", label: "used daily by the bakery's staff" },
          { value: "56 dishes", label: "real menu, 3 languages" },
          { value: "27 PRs", label: "160 commits, built solo" },
        ]}
        stack={["Go", "PostgreSQL", "JavaScript", "Telegram Mini Apps", "OSRM", "Railway", "Claude Code"]}
        links={[
          { label: "Showcase on GitHub", href: "https://github.com/LicBoy/rahat-go-showcase" },
          { label: "Watch the 80-second demo", href: "/media/rahat-go-demo.mp4" },
        ]}
      >
        <Fragment slot="story">
          <p>
            My friend owns a bakery in Da Nang. Orders came in through chats, stock lived on paper, and nobody knew
            which dishes actually made money. I built him one app that does all of it, inside Telegram, where his
            customers already are.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Customers</strong> browse the menu, order for pickup or delivery and pay by QR.{" "}
            <strong>Staff</strong> run the order pipeline, a POS, dine-in tables and daily stock.{" "}
            <strong>The owner</strong> sees revenue, best sellers and the real cost and margin of every dish.
          </p>
          <p>
            One Go binary serves the bot, the REST API and the Mini App. I designed it and built it with Claude Code
            sub-agents, reviewing every pull request.
          </p>
          <p class="try">Try it: add something to the cart, then switch to <em>Staff</em> and <em>Owner</em>.</p>
        </Fragment>
        <RahatDemo slot="demo" />
      </Chapter>

      <Chapter
        id="payments"
        num="02"
        kicker="Day job · 2023 — now"
        title="Test automation for instant payments"
        accent="#1f9d55"
        flip
        stats={[
          { value: "4–6×", label: "faster smoke and E2E testing" },
          { value: "6 testers", label: "onboarded to writing automated scenarios" },
        ]}
        stack={["Kotlin", "Cucumber / Gherkin", "Python", "pytest", "WireMock", "Jenkins", "Allure", "ISO 20022", "SWIFT MT"]}
        note="The scenario in the demo is illustrative and uses public ISO 20022 message types. Client systems and data are under NDA."
      >
        <Fragment slot="story">
          <p>
            At CMA Small Systems I build backend test frameworks for payment systems: instant payments, SWIFT MT and
            ISO 20022 MX messages, and the conversion between them.
          </p>
          <p>
            The trick that paid off most was writing the steps <strong>in business language</strong>. Our manual
            testers could read them, then write new scenarios themselves. I onboarded six of them, and testing that
            used to take days across several projects now takes hours.
          </p>
          <p class="try">Try it: run the scenario, or break the payment and watch the test catch it.</p>
        </Fragment>
        <PaymentsDemo slot="demo" />
      </Chapter>

      <Chapter
        id="labeling"
        num="03"
        kicker="ML + automation · 2023"
        title="I automated my own data-labeling job"
        accent="#3d7cf2"
        stats={[
          { value: "5,808", label: "chats I labeled by hand, used as training data" },
          { value: "77%", label: "accuracy on 379 hand-checked chats" },
        ]}
        stack={["Python", "PyTorch", "RuBERT-tiny", "Hugging Face", "Selenium", "Tampermonkey"]}
        links={[{ label: "Code on GitHub", href: "https://github.com/LicBoy/chatGPT-avito-solver" }]}
        note="Chats in the demo are made up; the numbers are from the project's saved results."
      >
        <Fragment slot="story">
          <p>
            In 2023 I had a part-time job labeling buyer–seller chats: <em>did they agree on a deal?</em> Yes, no, or
            not enough information. After a few thousand of them, I noticed I was the training data.
          </p>
          <p>
            A Tampermonkey script saved every answer I gave. I fine-tuned a small Russian BERT on those answers, and a
            Selenium bot used the model to read each chat and click the answer.
          </p>
        </Fragment>
        <LabelingDemo slot="demo" />
      </Chapter>

      <Chapter
        id="superres"
        num="04"
        kicker="Bachelor's thesis · 2022"
        title="Making small images sharp again with GANs"
        accent="#8b5cf6"
        flip
        stats={[
          { value: "4×", label: "upscaling, 252 px → 1008 px" },
          { value: "2 GANs", label: "SRGAN and ESRGAN, trained on CelebA" },
        ]}
        stack={["PyTorch", "GANs", "VGG19 perceptual loss", "Computer vision"]}
        links={[{ label: "Thesis code and results", href: "https://github.com/LicBoy/thesis-SR" }]}
      >
        <Fragment slot="story">
          <p>
            Classical upscaling can only blend the pixels it already has, so a 4× enlargement comes out blurry.
            A GAN learns what real detail looks like and draws it back in.
          </p>
          <p>
            I implemented and trained SRGAN and ESRGAN in PyTorch and compared them with bicubic interpolation.
            Drag the slider: left is bicubic, right is my ESRGAN, from the same small input.
          </p>
        </Fragment>
        <SrDemo slot="demo" />
      </Chapter>

      <Chapter
        id="swiper"
        num="05"
        kicker="Mobile game · 2020 — 2021"
        title="Swiper: my first released game"
        accent="#ff7a59"
        dark
        stack={["Unity", "C#", "Google AdMob", "Photoshop"]}
        links={[{ label: "C# source on GitHub", href: "https://github.com/LicBoy/2D-Game-Swiper-" }]}
        note="This is a small browser remake of the core loop. The original Android game was on Google Play until the developer account went inactive."
      >
        <Fragment slot="story">
          <p>
            Asteroids fall on your city; swipe to cut them down. Chain three or more in one swipe for a combo.
            I made it in Unity with C#: waves, 12 power-ups, dark and light themes, English and Russian, and AdMob ads.
          </p>
          <p class="try">Play a round. Desktop: drag with the mouse. Phone: swipe.</p>
        </Fragment>
        <SwiperDemo slot="demo" />
      </Chapter>
    </div>

    <Experience />
    <Services />
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Base>

<style>
  .nav { position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: 50; background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--paper) 86%, transparent); backdrop-filter: blur(10px); border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .row { display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; height: 58px; }
  .logo { font: 650 19px/1 var(--font-display); text-decoration: none; display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8px; }
  .tick { display: grid; place-items: center; width: 26px; height: 26px; border-radius: 8px; background: var(--ink); color: #3ccf7a; font-size: 15px; }
  .nav ul { list-style: none; display: flex; gap: clamp(14px, 3vw, 28px); margin: 0; padding: 0; }
  .nav a { text-decoration: none; font-weight: 550; font-size: 15px; }
  .nav ul a:hover { text-decoration: underline; text-underline-offset: 4px; }
  :global(.try) { font-weight: 600; color: var(--ink) !important; }
  :global(.dark .try) { color: #fff !important; }
</style>
